{"event":"start","run":"F3-Paka-1","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","effort":"medium","system":"You write one program in Paka. Reply with the whole file main.paka in exactly one\ncode block. Any other text is allowed; only the code block is used. A reply with no\ncode block or with several counts as a failed submission.\nThe harness builds it with: paka check main.paka\nIt runs the program on cases. Input comes as files named in the arguments.\nOutput is standard output, the exit status and, where the task says so, written files.\nThere is no standard input.\nAfter each submission you get the build result or the case results.\nYou have 8 submissions.\nUse only what Paka itself ships with: no outside packages.\nThe language reference follows.\n\n# Paka language reference\n\nAll of Paka is on this page, except visual apps: `spec/screens.md`. A block fenced as `paka` is a\nwhole program that `paka check` accepts. Any other block is a fragment, refused code or tool output.\n\n## 1. A program\n\n```paka\ntype Tally { words: Int = 0 }\n\nenum Msg { Start(path: String), Loaded(text: String), Failed(why: String) }\n\nfn count_words(text: String) -> Int {\n  len(words(text))\n}\n\nfn update(s: inout Tally, m: Msg) -> List<Effect<Msg>> {\n  match m {\n    Start(path) => [ReadFile(path, then: Loaded, or: Failed)]\n    Loaded(text) => {\n      s.words = count_words(text)\n      [Print(\"{s.words} words\"), Exit]\n    }\n    Failed(why) => [Print(\"cannot read file: {why}\"), ExitWith(20)]\n  }\n}\n\napp wc {\n  state: Tally\n  start: Start\n  update: update\n  grants: [ReadFiles, Console]\n}\n```\n\nA program is one `.paka` file, or every `.paka` file directly in one directory. It holds\ndefinitions in any order: `type`, `enum`, `fn`, `example` and at most one `app`. Definition,\nvariant and built-in names share one pool; each exists once (`duplicate-name`), but a definition\nor variant may take a built-in function's or element's name and replace it everywhere (`fn join`,\n`type Row`); a view then using that element is `duplicate-name`. An app may take any name.\n\nPaka has no user generics, interfaces, methods, lambdas, closures, tuples, null, exceptions,\nglobals, references or shared data. Functions cannot see anything but their parameters.\n\n## 2. Source text\n\n- UTF-8. Non-ASCII characters only inside strings and comments. No tab, no carriage return.\n- Names are ASCII letters, digits and `_`, and start with a letter. Upper first letter: types,\n  variants, effects, grants. Lower: functions, apps, examples, locals, parameters, fields, labels.\n- Keywords, never names: `app break continue else enum example false fn for foreign if in inout let\n  match migrate return true type var view while`.\n- `//` starts a comment that runs to the line end. No block comments.\n- A line break ends a statement. A line continues only inside an open `(` or `[`, after a line\n  ending in `,` `=` `->` `=>` `|` `{` or a two-sided operator, or before a line starting with\n  `}` `)` `]`. So: no `;` after a statement, `else` stands on the line of the `}` before it, and\n  `{ x }` may stand on one line. Fields and variants are separated by `,` or by a line break.\n- No trailing comma anywhere.\n- Nesting is at most 64 levels: each open `(` `[` `{`, type `<` and string `{`, and an `if` or\n  `match` in the condition or subject of another, and a `fn` type as the result of another. A\n  chain of operators, calls, fields or `?` has at most 1,000 links. Beyond either:\n  `nesting-too-deep`.\n\n## 3. Types\n\n| Type | Written | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| `Int` | `0` `42` `-7` | 64-bit signed |\n| `Float` | `1.5` `0.0` | IEEE 64-bit; digits on both sides of the point |\n| `Bool` | `true` `false` | |\n| `String` | `\"text\"` | a row of Unicode code points; one line |\n| `List<T>` | `[a, b]` `[]` | |\n| `Map<K, V>` | `[\"a\": 1]` `[:]` | K is `Int` or `String`; entries always in rising key order |\n| `Option<T>` | `Some(value)` `None` | |\n| `Result<T, E>` | `Ok(value)` `Err(error)` | E is any type |\n| `Effect<Msg>` | section 10 | |\n| `fn(A, B) -> R` | section 8 | a function type |\n\nNo Set (write `Map<K, Bool>`), no character type (a one-character `String`).\nNumber literals have no leading zero, hex, exponent or `_`.\n\n```\ntype Counter { count: Int = 0, name: String }   // record\nenum Shape { Dot, Circle(r: Float), Rect(w: Float, h: Float) }\nCounter { name: \"a\" }      // fields by name in declared order; one with a default may be left out\nc.count                    // field read\nCircle(1.5)                // variant fields by position; a variant without fields is written Dot\n```\n\n- Variants are written bare, never `Shape.Dot`, and a variant name is unique in the whole program.\n  An enum has at least one variant; variant fields have no defaults.\n- A default is a literal, `[]`, `[:]`, or a list or variant of defaults; anything else is\n  `unexpected-token`.\n- In the header of `if`, `while`, `for` and `match`, write a record in brackets: `(Counter {})`.\n- A type may hold itself only where a finite value exists: through `Option<T>`, a `List`, or an\n  enum with another variant (`enum Expr { Num(value: Int), Add(left: Expr, right: Expr) }`).\n  Otherwise `no-finite-value`.\n- Types are written on every parameter, result and field. A local takes the type of its value:\n  `let n = 1`. Values typed by their position (`[]`, `[:]`, `None`, `Ok(x)`, `Err(e)`, a hole,\n  `unreachable()`) need a parameter, field, result, operand or annotation to give the type, else\n  `cannot-infer`: write `let xs: List<Int> = []`. An `if` or `match` with no expected type takes\n  the type of its first branch that has one.\n\n## 4. Functions, statements, expressions\n\n```paka\ntype Stack { items: List<Int> = [] }\n\nenum Shape { Dot, Circle(r: Float), Rect(w: Float, h: Float) }\n\nfn clamp(x: Int; lo: Int, hi: Int) -> Int {\n  max(lo, min(x, hi))\n}\n\nfn area(s: Shape) -> Float {\n  match s {\n    Dot => 0.0\n    Circle(r) => r * r * 3.14159\n    Rect(w, h) => w * h\n  }\n}\n\nfn push(s: inout Stack, x: Int) {\n  append(&s.items, x)\n}\n\nfn sum_odd(n: Int) -> Int {\n  var total = 0\n  for i in 0..<n {\n    if i % 2 == 0 {\n      continue\n    }\n    total += i\n  }\n  total\n}\n\nfn sign(n: Int) -> String {\n  if n < 0 { \"minus\" } else if n == 0 { \"zero\" } else { \"plus\" }\n}\n\nexample tour {\n  var st = Stack {}\n  push(&st, clamp(12, lo: 0, hi: 9))\n  let r = Rect(2.0, 3.0)\n  st.items == [9] && area(r) == 6.0 && sum_odd(5) == 4 && sign(-1) == \"minus\"\n}\n```\n\n- Parameters before `;` are passed by position; those after it as `name: value`, in declared\n  order. A call writes exactly those labels. `f()` calls a function without parameters.\n- A function without `-> T` gives no value.\n- The value of a block is its last line. `return value` only leaves early, and a bare `return`\n  leaves a function without a result; on the last line it is the error `return-last`: drop\n  `return`.\n- `let x = e` is fixed; `var x = e` can be assigned. Either may carry a type: `let x: T = e`.\n- No shadowing: a `let`, `var`, parameter, `for` name or pattern name may not reuse a local name\n  visible there (`duplicate-local`). Blocks side by side may reuse a name. A local may hide a\n  function name.\n- Assignment: `place = e`, also `+= -= *= /= %=`. A place is a `var` local or an inout parameter,\n  then any `.field` and `[index]` steps: `g[r][c] = v`, `s.items[i].count += 1`.\n- `if c { } else if d { } else { }`. Used as a value it needs `else`.\n- `while c { }`; `for x in xs { }` walks a List or an Int range `a..<b` (b not included) and\n  nothing else: write `for c in chars(text)`, `for k in keys(m)`. `break`, `continue`.\n- `match`: one arm per line, no commas. A pattern is flat: `V`, `V(a, _)` (fields by position,\n  each a new name or `_`), an Int, a String, `true`, `false` or `_`. `A | B => ...` joins\n  patterns and binds no names. A match on an enum names every variant; a whole-pattern `_` is\n  allowed only when the subject is an Int, String or Bool. An arm is an expression, an assignment\n  or a block; `V => {}` does nothing.\n- A value must be used. A statement whose value is dropped is `unused-result`; write\n  `_ = pop(&q)`. A call that gives no value stands alone, without `_ =`.\n- An unused local is only a warning.\n- A statement after `return`, `break`, `continue` or `unreachable()` in the same block is the\n  error `unreachable-code`.\n\nOperators, tightest first: call, index, `.field`, `?`; unary `-` `!`; `*` `/` `%`; `+` `-`;\n`..<`; `==` `!=` `<` `<=` `>` `>=`; `&&`; `||`. Comparisons do not chain: write\n`a < b && b < c`. Evaluation is strictly left to right; `&&` and `||` skip the right side when the\nleft decides.\n\nBoth sides of an operator have the same type; nothing converts by itself (`to_float(n)`,\n`to_int(x)`).\n\n| Operator | Types |\n|---|---|\n| `==` `!=` | every type, compared by structure |\n| `<` `<=` `>` `>=` | Int, Float, String (by code point) |\n| `+` `+=` | Int, Float, String (joins) |\n| `-` `*` `/` | Int, Float |\n| `%` | Int |\n| `!` `&&` `\\|\\|` | Bool |\n\n## 5. Numbers and text\n\n- An Int result outside -9223372036854775808 to 9223372036854775807 traps `overflow`. `/`\n  truncates toward zero, `%` takes the sign of the left side, a zero divisor traps\n  `divide_by_zero`. Wrapping and bit operations are functions (section 9).\n- Float arithmetic never traps. NaN equals nothing, itself included.\n- String escapes, exactly these seven, no `\\u{hex}`: `\\n` `\\r` `\\t` `\\\\` `\\\"` `\\{` `\\}`. A\n  literal `{` is written `\\{`. A lone `}` and a `{` before the closing quote are text: `\"}\"`, `\"{\"`.\n- `\"{expr}\"` puts an Int, Bool or String into the text. Strings inside it hold no `{`:\n  `\"{join(xs, \" \")}\"`. A Float is written `\"{fixed(x, digits: 2)}\"`. No other type becomes text\n  by itself: build the text from its parts.\n- `s[i]` is a one-character String, `s[a..<b]` and `xs[a..<b]` are slices, each a new value.\n  Positions start at 0 and count code points; `len` counts the same. A position outside traps\n  `out_of_bounds`.\n- A String changes only as a whole: `s += \"x\"`. `s[i] = \"x\"` is refused.\n- A digit test: `c >= \"0\" && c <= \"9\"`.\n\n## 6. Values and `inout`\n\n- Every local, field and item is its own value. `let b = a` copies in meaning: changing one never\n  changes the other. Two places never share data.\n- Arguments are read-only. To let a function change the caller's data, declare the parameter\n  `inout` and mark the argument with `&`: `fn push(s: inout Stack, x: Int)`, `push(&st, 1)`.\n- `&` is written on every inout argument and nowhere else. After it comes a `var` local or an\n  inout parameter with any field and index steps (`&g.adj[a]`, `&m[key]`). Not a `let` (declare\n  it `var`), a read-only parameter (declare it `inout`), a `for` name or pattern name (they are\n  copies), a slice (pass the list and the positions) or a String position.\n- Two places overlap unless they start at different variables or first differ at two different\n  field names. Two index steps into one List or Map always overlap: `xs[i]` and `xs[j]`.\n- `inout-overlap`: a place passed with `&` overlaps another argument of the same call.\n  Fix: bind it first, `let old = xs` then `grow(&xs, old)`. For two items of one list, bind one\n  first or pass the list and both positions. Int, Float and Bool arguments are exempt; a String is not.\n- `changed-while-read`: one operand reads a place that a sibling operand changes. This holds for\n  operators, list, map and record literals and interpolation too. Fix: bind it first.\n- `changed-while-walked`: the body of `for x in xs` changes `xs` or anything overlapping it.\n  Fix: walk a copy (`let items = xs`) or walk by position (`for i in 0..<len(xs)`). `while`\n  freezes nothing.\n- The fields of a variant are not places. To change one, match, build the new value and assign\n  the subject: `Rect(w, h) => s = Rect(w + 1, h)`.\n- Trees are enums or records that hold themselves; using a subtree twice makes two copies.\n  Graphs use list positions: a node is its index. Build the adjacency lists with\n  `var adj: List<List<Int>> = list_of([], count: n)`; the annotation is needed (`cannot-infer`).\n\n```paka\ntype Graph { adj: List<List<Int>> = [] }\n\nfn dfs(g: Graph, seen: inout List<Bool>, u: Int) {\n  seen[u] = true\n  for v in g.adj[u] {\n    if !seen[v] { dfs(g, &seen, v) }\n  }\n}\n\nexample reach {\n  let g = Graph { adj: [[1], [0], []] }\n  var seen = list_of(false, count: 3)\n  dfs(g, &seen, 0)\n  seen == [true, true, false]\n}\n```\n\n## 7. Failure: `Option`, `Result`, `?`\n\n- A function that can fail or have no answer says so in its result: `Option<T>` or\n  `Result<T, E>`. Take them apart with `match`, `unwrap_or` or `?`.\n- Brackets claim, names ask. `xs[i]`, `m[key]` and `/` claim the position, key or divisor is\n  good, and trap when it is not. `get(m, key)`, `find`, `parse_int` and `parse_float` ask, and\n  give an `Option`.\n- `e?` gives the inside of `Some` or `Ok`; on `None` or `Err` the function returns that at once.\n  It may stand only in a function whose result is `Option<_>` (for an Option) or `Result<_, E>`\n  with the same `E` (for a Result). Not in `update`, not in an example.\n- An Option inside a Result function: `ok_or(opt, error)?`. A Result inside an Option function is\n  matched.\n- Changes made through an inout parameter before a `?` returns stay made.\n- An arm that returns, breaks or continues is a block: `None => {`, `return Err(\"empty\")`, `}` on three lines.\n- `unreachable()` stands where a value is expected but cannot be reached; it traps `assert`.\n- There is no `unwrap`, `expect`, `is_some`, `panic` or null.\n- A program reports a failure in `update`: `[Print(why), ExitWith(20)]`.\n\n```paka\nfn total(fields: List<String>) -> Result<Int, String> {\n  var sum = 0\n  for f in fields {\n    sum += ok_or(parse_int(f), \"not a number: {f}\")?\n  }\n  Ok(sum)\n}\n\nfn first_word(text: String) -> Option<String> {\n  let ws = words(text)\n  if len(ws) == 0 {\n    return None\n  }\n  Some(ws[0])\n}\n\nfn count_words(text: String) -> Map<String, Int> {\n  var counts: Map<String, Int> = [:]\n  for w in words(text) {\n    counts[w] = unwrap_or(get(counts, w), 0) + 1\n  }\n  counts\n}\n\nexample failures {\n  total([\"1\", \"2\"]) == Ok(3) && total([\"x\"]) == Err(\"not a number: x\") &&\n    first_word(\"\") == None && count_words(\"a b a\") == [\"a\": 2, \"b\": 1]\n}\n```\n\n## 8. Function values\n\n- A function value is the bare name of a user `fn` without inout parameters, or of a user variant\n  with fields (`Loaded` is a `fn(String) -> Msg`). Its type is written `fn(A, B) -> R`.\n- A call through a function value is positional and takes no `&`.\n- Built-in functions and built-in variants with fields are not values (`not-a-value`): wrap one in\n  a user `fn`. `None` and `Exit` are plain values.\n- There are no lambdas. Write a top-level function and pass its name:\n\n```\nfn by_score(a: Player, b: Player) -> Bool { a.score > b.score }\nsort_by(&players, by_score)\n```\n\n- Two function values are equal when they name the same definition.\n- An app's state may hold no function type.\n\n## 9. Built-in functions\n\n`T`, `K`, `V`, `E` stand for any type. All arguments are positional except the three labels\nshown (`digits:`, `at:`, `count:`); a `;` in a row only marks where the labels start, a call\nwrites `,`. A row without `->` gives no value. A row that changes its first argument takes it with `&`.\n\n```\nlen(x) -> Int                         String: code points; List: items; Map: entries\nis_empty(x) -> Bool                   len(x) == 0\ncontains(text, part) -> Bool          also contains(xs, item)\nfind(text, part) -> Option<Int>       first position; also find(xs, item)\n\nmin(a, b) -> T   max(a, b) -> T       Int, Float or String\nabs(x) -> T                           Int or Float\nto_float(n: Int) -> Float\nto_int(x: Float) -> Int               drops the fraction; traps bad_conversion on NaN or out of range\nsqrt(x) floor(x) ceil(x) trunc(x) round(x) -> Float    round: ties away from zero\nfixed(x: Float; digits: Int) -> String        digits 0 to 20; ties away from zero\nwrap_add(a, b) wrap_sub(a, b) wrap_mul(a, b) -> Int   wraps instead of trapping\nbit_and(a, b) bit_or(a, b) bit_xor(a, b) bit_not(a) -> Int\nshift_left(a, count) shift_right(a, count) -> Int      count 0 to 63\n\nparse_int(text) -> Option<Int>        \"-12\", \"007\"; nothing trimmed; None when not a number\nparse_float(text) -> Option<Float>\ncode_at(text, i) -> Int               the code point at position i\nfrom_code(n) -> String                one character\n\nsplit(text, sep) -> List<String>      split(\"a,,b\", \",\") is [\"a\", \"\", \"b\"]\nchars(text) -> List<String>           one String per character\nlines(text) -> List<String>           split on \"\\n\"; no empty last piece\nwords(text) -> List<String>           runs between white space, never empty\njoin(parts, sep) -> String\nrepeat(text, n) -> String\npad_left(text, width) -> String       spaces in front up to width\npad_right(text, width) -> String\ntrim(text) trim_start(text) trim_end(text) -> String     white space off the ends\nstarts_with(text, part) ends_with(text, part) -> Bool\nreplace(text, old, new) -> String     every match\nupper(text) lower(text) -> String     ASCII letters only\n\nappend(&xs, item)\nextend(&xs, more)                     appends every item of the List more\npop(&xs) -> T                         removes the last item; traps on an empty List\ninsert(&xs, item; at: Int)            at == len(xs) appends. Call: insert(&xs, x, at: 0)\nremove_at(&xs, i) -> T\nswap(&xs, i, j)\nreverse(&xs)\nsort(&xs)                             rising; List of Int, Float or String\nsort_by(&xs, before)                  before: fn(T, T) -> Bool\nlist_of(item; count: Int) -> List<T>  count copies\n\nget(m, key) -> Option<V>\nhas(m, key) -> Bool\nremove(&m, key)                       a missing key changes nothing\nkeys(m) -> List<K>                    in rising order\nvalues(m) -> List<V>                  in key order\n\nunwrap_or(opt, fallback) -> T         the inside of Some, else fallback\nok_or(opt, error) -> Result<T, E>     Some(x) gives Ok(x), None gives Err(error)\nassert(cond)                          traps assert when false\nunreachable()                         traps assert when reached\n```\n\n- White space is exactly six characters: code points 9 to 13 and 32.\n- Text is matched exactly, left to right, without overlap. No patterns.\n- The sort is stable: `before(a, b)` says whether `a` must come before `b`; ties keep their\n  order.\n- Map: `m[key]` reads and traps `out_of_bounds` when the key is missing. `m[key] = v` adds or\n  replaces. Other uses need the key present: `m[key] += 1` on a new key traps. The count idiom\n  is `counts[w] = unwrap_or(get(counts, w), 0) + 1`.\n- A bad position, a count below zero, `pop` on an empty List, a shift count or `digits` out of\n  range trap `out_of_bounds`.\n- There is no `map`, `filter`, `fold`, `sum`, `sorted` or `clamp`: loop.\n\n## 10. Apps and effects\n\n```\napp NAME {\n  state: S          // optional: a record with a default on every field, else `bad-state`\n  start: Start      // required: a variant of the message enum; its fields are all String\n  update: update    // required: the handler\n  grants: [Console] // required, may be []: Console, ReadFiles, WriteFiles\n}\n```\n\nOne key per line. The handler has exactly one of two shapes:\n\n```\nfn update(s: inout S, m: Msg) -> List<Effect<Msg>>     // with state:\nfn update(m: Msg) -> List<Effect<Msg>>                 // without state:\n```\n\n| Effect | Needs grant | Answer |\n|---|---|---|\n| `Print(text)` | `Console` | none; writes the text and a line feed to standard output |\n| `ReadFile(path; then: fn(String) -> Msg, or: fn(String) -> Msg)` | `ReadFiles` | `then(text)` or `or(reason)` |\n| `WriteFile(path, text; then: Msg, or: fn(String) -> Msg)` | `WriteFiles` | `then` or `or(reason)` |\n| `Exit` | none | ends with status 0 |\n| `ExitWith(status)` | none | ends with that status: 0 or 5 to 255 |\n\n`WriteFile`'s `then:` is a message value that may carry context:\n`WriteFile(path, text, then: Saved(path), or: Failed)`.\n\nEffects are plain values; only the list `update` returns is performed. `then:` and `or:`\nare both required. A reason is one of `not found`, `no permission`, `not a file`, `not text`,\n`no space`, `failed`. `WriteFile` replaces the whole file; `ReadFile` reads it all.\n\nHow a program runs:\n\n- The command-line arguments fill the fields of the `start:` variant by position. A wrong count\n  ends the run with status 2 and a usage line such as `wc: expected 1 argument (path), got 0`.\n  A first argument `--paka-examples` or `--paka-replay` is the tool's: unless served, status 2 too.\n- The state starts from its defaults; the start message is first in the queue.\n- The runtime takes the front message and calls `update`. When the handler has returned, its\n  effects are performed in list order. Each file answer joins the back of the queue.\n- Answers arrive in the order their effects were performed. Answers carry no path: keep in the\n  state what you wait for.\n- `Exit` and `ExitWith` end the program at once. Later effects are not performed, and a queued\n  answer is dropped.\n- A trap in a handler performs none of that handler's effects.\n- There is no standard input: data comes through arguments and files.\n\nFour stops end a program with one line on standard error and status 3:\n\n```\n<app>: effect <Effect> refused: grant <Grant> is missing\n<app>: effect Print failed: <reason>\n<app>: effect ExitWith failed: status <n> is not 0 or 5 to 255\n<app>: ended without Exit\n```\n\nA grant is tested as its effect is performed. Every path must reach `Exit` or\n`ExitWith`: an empty queue without one is the fourth stop. A literal status outside 0 or 5 to 255\nis the check error `literal-out-of-range`. A program without an app checks; `paka run` and\n`paka build` refuse it (`no-app`).\n\n## 11. Traps, holes, examples\n\nA trap ends the program with status 3. It cannot be caught. Evaluation order decides which trap\ncomes first. The eight kinds:\n\n| Kind | Raised by |\n|---|---|\n| `overflow` | Int arithmetic out of range |\n| `divide_by_zero` | Int `/` or `%` by 0 |\n| `out_of_bounds` | a bad index, slice, key, count or position |\n| `bad_conversion` | `to_int`, `from_code` |\n| `assert` | `assert(false)`, `unreachable()` |\n| `hole` | a hole reached |\n| `stack` | more than 10,000 nested calls |\n| `memory` | a List, Map or String beyond 16,777,216 items, entries or bytes |\n\n```\n<app>: trap <kind> at <file>:<line>:<col>\n  <detail>\n  in <function>, called from <function>\n```\n\nThe kind and the position are meaning; the two indented lines are help and may be absent.\n\nA hole `??name` stands for code not yet written; it takes the type its position expects.\n`paka check` accepts it (a note, status 0), reaching it traps `hole`, and `paka build` refuses it.\n\nAn example is `example name { ... }`: statements, then one Bool expression. It takes no\narguments, performs no effects and may not use `?`. Examples may call `update` on a `var` state\nand compare effect lists with `==`. They are checked with the program and run by\n`paka examples`. Each gets one status: `pass` (true), `fail` (false), `trapped` (a trap) or\n`unfinished` (a hole reached). Its line reads name, label, status; the label is `human-owned` when\nthe signed approval store holds it, else `agent-written`.\n\n## 12. Tools\n\n| Command | Does |\n|---|---|\n| `paka check <path> [--json]` | checks; prints the error, or warnings and hole notes |\n| `paka run <path> [arguments]` | checks, then runs the app |\n| `paka examples <path>` | checks, then runs every example |\n| `paka build <path> -o <output>` | checks, then writes an executable |\n| `paka gate <tree> --base <tree> [--json]` | refuses a merge whose approvals differ or owned examples fail; lists each |\n| `paka approve <path>` | the owner's: signs approved changes |\n\nExit status: 0 good; 1 a program error; 2 bad usage; 3 a trap, a stop or an example that\ndid not pass; 4 an internal error.\n\n`paka build` lists every hole, one line each; the others report only the first error, as one\nline. `paka check` adds one line per field:\n\n```\nmain.paka:4:3: error[type-mismatch]: expected `List<Effect<Msg>>`, found `String`\n  definition: update\n  bytes: 63..83\n  text: \"\\\"let there be light\\\"\"\n  expected: List<Effect<Msg>>\n  found: String\n```\n\nA `fix:` line follows when the error knows its repair. With `--json` the same error is one object\nwith the keys `kind`, `definition`, `message`, `file`, `start`, `end`, `line`, `column`, `text`,\n`expected`, `found`, `fix`. Lines and columns start at 1; columns count bytes.\n\n## 16. Foreign code\n\n```\nforeign Sum {\n  source: \"sum.c\"   // or library: \"z\"; beside the program\n  header: \"sum.h\"\n  Add(a: Int) -> Int = \"add\"\n}\n```\n\nEach line is an effect under grant `Sum`, answered like `ReadFile`; `then: Msg` with no result.\nArguments: Int, Float, Bool, String; result: Int, Float, Bool. The owner approves the grant,\ndeclaration and files. For now it answers `or(\"no host\")`.\n","at":1794834274326}
{"event":"call","n":1,"sent":"# Patch apply\n\nApply an edit script to a text file and write the result to a new file.\n\n## Arguments\n\nThe program is run with exactly three arguments: the target name, the script name and the output\nname. Each is 1 to 30 characters, each a letter `A` to `Z` or `a` to `z`, a digit `0` to `9`, `.`,\n`_`, `-` or `/`. The output name is never the script name, but it may be the target name: then the\ntarget is replaced by the result.\n\n## Files\n\nBoth input files hold only the characters with codes 32 to 126 and the line feed (code 10). Each is\na sequence of lines: each line ends at a line feed, and the last line may lack one. An empty file\nhas no lines. Lines are numbered from 1. Each file has at most 200 lines of at most 200 characters.\nLet `n` be the number of lines of the target.\n\n## Script\n\nEvery line of the script is a command or a text line. There are no blank lines: an empty line, or\none of only spaces, is a bad line (see Errors).\n\nA number is `0`, or a digit `1` to `9` followed by at most three digits `0` to `9`. So `07` and\n`10000` are not numbers. A command is one of these, with exactly one space after the letter and\nnothing else on the line, `A` and `B` numbers:\n\n- `d A` or `d A-B`: delete lines `A` to `B` of the target (`d A` is `d A-A`).\n- `c A` or `c A-B`: change lines `A` to `B` of the target: delete them and put the text lines of\n  the command in their place.\n- `a A`: add the text lines of the command after line `A` of the target; `a 0` adds them before\n  line 1.\n\nFor `d` and `c`, `A` is at least 1 and `B` is at least `A`. A text line is `>` alone, which stands\nfor an empty line, or `> ` (a `>` and a space) followed by zero or more characters, which stand for\nthose characters exactly: `>   x` stands for `  x`, and `> > y` for `> y`. The text lines of a `c` or\n`a` command are the text lines that follow it directly, up to the next command or the end of the\nscript; it has at least one. A `d` command has none.\n\nThe places of the target are, in order: gap 0, line 1, gap 1, line 2, and so on up to line `n` and\ngap `n`. Gap `g` is the place between line `g` and line `g + 1`. A `d` or `c` command takes lines\n`A` to `B` and the gaps between them; an `a` command takes gap `A`. Every command must take only\nplaces that come after every place taken by the command before it in the script. So after `d 3-5`,\n`a 5` and `d 6` may follow but `a 4` may not; after `a 5`, `d 6` may follow but `a 5` and `c 5` may\nnot.\n\n## Output\n\nThe result is the target with every command applied, all of them to the target as read. Write it\nto the output file: every line ends with a line feed, but one. When the last line of the target\nlacks a line feed, is not deleted or changed, and no line is added after it, it is also the last\nline of the result, and it lacks a line feed there too. A file of the same name that already\nexists is replaced entirely. Then print one line and exit with status 0:\n\n```\nwrote K lines to OUTPUT (+I -D)\n```\n\n`K` is the number of lines of the result, `I` the number of text lines in the script and `D` the\nnumber of target lines deleted or changed, all in decimal. When `K` is 1 the word is `line`.\n\nExample: run with `notes.txt`, `fix.txt` and `new.txt`, where `notes.txt` holds\n\n```\none\ntwo\nthree\nfour\n```\n\nand `fix.txt` holds\n\n```\nd 2\nc 3\n> THREE\n> 3.5\na 4\n> five\n```\n\nThe file `new.txt` is written with the lines `one`, `THREE`, `3.5`, `four` and `five`, and the\noutput is:\n\n```\nwrote 5 lines to new.txt (+3 -2)\n```\n\n## Errors\n\nEach error prints exactly one line, ending with a line feed, and nothing else, and exits with\nstatus 20. `N` below is a line number of the script in decimal. The checks are made in this order\nand only the first that fails is reported:\n\n1. The script cannot be read, for example because it does not exist: `error: cannot read NAME`,\n   with the script name.\n2. The script lines are checked in order from line 1, and only the first line with an error is\n   reported. On that line, the checks are made in this order:\n   1. It is not a command or a text line: `error: script line N: bad line`. So `a 1-2`, `d 0`,\n      `>x` and `D 1` are bad lines.\n   2. It is a `d` or `c` command with `B` less than `A`: `error: script line N: backward range`\n   3. It is a text line that does not follow a `c` or `a` command or one of its text lines:\n      `error: script line N: unexpected text`\n   4. It is a `c` or `a` command and the next line is not a text line, or there is no next line:\n      `error: script line N: missing text`\n3. The target cannot be read: `error: cannot read NAME`, with the target name.\n4. The commands are checked in script order, and only the first command with an error is reported,\n   with `N` its line. On that command, the checks are made in this order:\n   1. It names a line after line `n` (for `a`, `A` is more than `n`): `error: script line N: past\n      end`\n   2. It takes a place that does not come after every place taken by the command before it:\n      `error: script line N: out of order`\n5. The output cannot be written, for example because its name names a folder that does not exist:\n   `error: cannot write NAME`, with the output name.\n\nWhen a check of 1 to 4 fails, no file is written. The program never creates a folder.\n\n## Visible cases\n\n### Case 1\n\nArguments: \"notes.txt\" \"fix.txt\" \"new.txt\"\n\nFile `fix.txt`:\n```\nd 2\nc 3\n> THREE\n> 3.5\na 4\n> five\n```\n\nFile `notes.txt`:\n```\none\ntwo\nthree\nfour\n```\n\nExpected standard output:\n```\nwrote 5 lines to new.txt (+3 -2)\n```\n\nExpected written file `new.txt`:\n```\none\nTHREE\n3.5\nfour\nfive\n```\n\nExpected exit status: 0\n\n### Case 2\n\nArguments: \"list.txt\" \"s.txt\" \"out/list.txt\"\n\nFile `list.txt`:\n```\na\nb\n```\n\nFile `s.txt`:\n```\na 2\n> c\n```\n\nExpected standard output:\n```\nerror: cannot write out/list.txt\n```\n\nExpected exit status: 20\n","at":1794834274330}
{"event":"reply","n":1,"text":"This is my first submission and I haven't run `paka check` on it, so it may still have build errors. The program reads the script, parses and checks it, then reads the target. It then checks the commands against the target and writes the result with a `WriteFile` effect. A missing final line feed is kept only when the target's last line is copied through unchanged with nothing added after it.\n\n```paka\ntype Cmd { kind: String, a: Int, b: Int, line: Int, text: List<String> }\n\ntype State {\n  target: String = \"\"\n  script: String = \"\"\n  out: String = \"\"\n  cmds: List<Cmd> = []\n}\n\ntype Built { lines: List<String>, tail: Bool, del: Int }\n\nenum Msg {\n  Start(target: String, script: String, out: String),\n  ScriptOk(text: String),\n  ScriptBad(why: String),\n  TargetOk(text: String),\n  TargetBad(why: String),\n  Written(summary: String),\n  WriteBad(why: String)\n}\n\nfn is_num(s: String) -> Bool {\n  let n = len(s)\n  if n == 0 || n > 4 {\n    return false\n  }\n  if n > 1 && s[0] == \"0\" {\n    return false\n  }\n  for c in chars(s) {\n    if !(c >= \"0\" && c <= \"9\") {\n      return false\n    }\n  }\n  true\n}\n\nfn is_text(line: String) -> Bool {\n  line == \">\" || starts_with(line, \"> \")\n}\n\nfn parse_cmd(line: String, ln: Int) -> Option<Cmd> {\n  if len(line) < 3 {\n    return None\n  }\n  let k = line[0]\n  if line[1] != \" \" {\n    return None\n  }\n  let rest = line[2..<len(line)]\n  if k == \"a\" {\n    if !is_num(rest) {\n      return None\n    }\n    let a = unwrap_or(parse_int(rest), 0)\n    return Some(Cmd { kind: \"a\", a: a, b: a, line: ln, text: [] })\n  }\n  if k != \"d\" && k != \"c\" {\n    return None\n  }\n  let parts = split(rest, \"-\")\n  if len(parts) > 2 {\n    return None\n  }\n  for p in parts {\n    if !is_num(p) {\n      return None\n    }\n  }\n  let a = unwrap_or(parse_int(parts[0]), 0)\n  var b = a\n  if len(parts) == 2 {\n    b = unwrap_or(parse_int(parts[1]), 0)\n  }\n  if a < 1 {\n    return None\n  }\n  Some(Cmd { kind: k, a: a, b: b, line: ln, text: [] })\n}\n\nfn parse_script(text: String) -> Result<List<Cmd>, String> {\n  let ls = lines(text)\n  var cmds: List<Cmd> = []\n  for i in 0..<len(ls) {\n    let line = ls[i]\n    let n = i + 1\n    if is_text(line) {\n      if len(cmds) == 0 {\n        return Err(\"error: script line {n}: unexpected text\")\n      }\n      let last = len(cmds) - 1\n      if cmds[last].kind == \"d\" {\n        return Err(\"error: script line {n}: unexpected text\")\n      }\n      var t = \"\"\n      if len(line) > 2 {\n        t = line[2..<len(line)]\n      }\n      append(&cmds[last].text, t)\n    } else {\n      match parse_cmd(line, n) {\n        None => {\n          return Err(\"error: script line {n}: bad line\")\n        }\n        Some(c) => {\n          if c.kind != \"a\" && c.b < c.a {\n            return Err(\"error: script line {n}: backward range\")\n          }\n          if c.kind != \"d\" {\n            if i + 1 >= len(ls) {\n              return Err(\"error: script line {n}: missing text\")\n            }\n            if !is_text(ls[i + 1]) {\n              return Err(\"error: script line {n}: missing text\")\n            }\n          }\n          append(&cmds, c)\n        }\n      }\n    }\n  }\n  Ok(cmds)\n}\n\nfn check_cmds(cmds: List<Cmd>, n: Int) -> Option<String> {\n  var prev = -1\n  for c in cmds {\n    let ln = c.line\n    let top = if c.kind == \"a\" { c.a } else { c.b }\n    if top > n {\n      return Some(\"error: script line {ln}: past end\")\n    }\n    var lo = 2 * c.a\n    var hi = 2 * c.a\n    if c.kind != \"a\" {\n      lo = 2 * c.a - 1\n      hi = 2 * c.b - 1\n    }\n    if lo <= prev {\n      return Some(\"error: script line {ln}: out of order\")\n    }\n    prev = hi\n  }\n  None\n}\n\nfn apply(cmds: List<Cmd>, t: List<String>) -> Built {\n  var res: List<String> = []\n  var cur = 1\n  var tail = false\n  var del = 0\n  let n = len(t)\n  for c in cmds {\n    let upto = if c.kind == \"a\" { c.a } else { c.a - 1 }\n    while cur <= upto {\n      append(&res, t[cur - 1])\n      if cur == n {\n        tail = true\n      }\n      cur += 1\n    }\n    if c.kind != \"a\" {\n      cur = c.b + 1\n      del += c.b - c.a + 1\n    }\n    if c.kind != \"d\" {\n      for s in c.text {\n        append(&res, s)\n      }\n      tail = false\n    }\n  }\n  while cur <= n {\n    append(&res, t[cur - 1])\n    if cur == n {\n      tail = true\n    }\n    cur += 1\n  }\n  Built { lines: res, tail: tail, del: del }\n}\n\nfn update(s: inout State, m: Msg) -> List<Effect<Msg>> {\n  match m {\n    Start(target, script, out) => {\n      s.target = target\n      s.script = script\n      s.out = out\n      [ReadFile(script, then: ScriptOk, or: ScriptBad)]\n    }\n    ScriptOk(text) => {\n      match parse_script(text) {\n        Err(e) => [Print(e), ExitWith(20)]\n        Ok(cmds) => {\n          s.cmds = cmds\n          [ReadFile(s.target, then: TargetOk, or: TargetBad)]\n        }\n      }\n    }\n    ScriptBad(_) => [Print(\"error: cannot read {s.script}\"), ExitWith(20)]\n    TargetBad(_) => [Print(\"error: cannot read {s.target}\"), ExitWith(20)]\n    TargetOk(text) => {\n      let t = lines(text)\n      match check_cmds(s.cmds, len(t)) {\n        Some(e) => [Print(e), ExitWith(20)]\n        None => {\n          let o = apply(s.cmds, t)\n          let nonl = len(text) > 0 && !ends_with(text, \"\\n\")\n          var body = \"\"\n          let cnt = len(o.lines)\n          for i in 0..<cnt {\n            body += o.lines[i]\n            let islast = i == cnt - 1\n            if !(islast && o.tail && nonl) {\n              body += \"\\n\"\n            }\n          }\n          var ins = 0\n          for c in s.cmds {\n            ins += len(c.text)\n          }\n          let word = if cnt == 1 { \"line\" } else { \"lines\" }\n          let del = o.del\n          let summary = \"wrote {cnt} {word} to {s.out} (+{ins} -{del})\"\n          [WriteFile(s.out, body, then: Written(summary), or: WriteBad)]\n        }\n      }\n    }\n    Written(sm) => [Print(sm), Exit]\n    WriteBad(_) => [Print(\"error: cannot write {s.out}\"), ExitWith(20)]\n  }\n}\n\napp patch {\n  state: State\n  start: Start\n  update: update\n  grants: [ReadFiles, WriteFiles, Console]\n}\n```","input":12617,"output":8598,"cut_off":false,"ms":60531,"at":1794834334864}
